  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The coastal flood risk will linger this week.\n\nThere is a continued risk for minor to near moderate coastal \nflooding through much of the week, mainly centered along the \ncoastal portions of Charleston and Colleton Counties. Coastal \nflooding will be near moderate levels both Tuesday and Wednesday\nmornings with minor flooding Thursday and possibly Friday \nmornings. Much of the guidance supports minor flooding during \nthis time, but tides have been consistently over performing, \npossibly due to some slightly slowing of the Gulf Stream \noffshore during the period of strong counter flow conditions \nexperienced several days ago. So, the forecast for the next few \nmornings remain slightly above the P-ETSS 10% given recent \ntrends. Right now, tides are expected to remain minor flood \nthresholds at Fort Pulaski. Fort Pulaski did reach minor flood \nlevels with this morning's high tide (09/28), but a timely \npassing ship was likely what pushed the gage just past 9.5 ft \nMLLW.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Warm and dry conditions will extend through most\nof the week.\n\nA departing trough will make way for an upper ridge axis \nshifting toward the Southeastern U.S. This will result in a \nrelatively stable, rain-free pattern. High temperatures will \nclimb into the mid to upper 80s daily, roughly 5 degrees above \nnormal for late Sept/early Oct, with parts of Southeast Georgia \nreaching the lower 90s. Expect ideal radiational cooling tonight\nunder clear skies and light winds, bringing lows down into the \nupper 50s inland and mid 60s closer to the coast. Lows will \ngradually warm each night after, hovering in the mid to upper \n60s inland and low to mid 70s near the beaches.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Unsettled weather could return late week, and \npossibly persisting through the weekend.\n\nThe next decent chance of rain will be Friday as a cold front \napproaches. Rain chances will further increase this weekend as \nthe front potentially stalls nearby and deep, tropical \ninfluenced moisture from the Gulf is ushered in. PWATs could \njump to an impressive 2.25 inches (near daily record levels) or \nhigher across the area. This event is still several days away so\ndetails are limited, but this could set the stage for heavy \ndownpours and a heightened risk of flooding.",
